        [2. Diagnostic Prototypes (Standard Reference)]:
        - [Melanoma NOS Standard]: Melanoma NOS exhibits a diverse array of visual features, yet refined diagnostic criteria emphasize irregular borders, poorly defined margins, heterogeneous pigment distribution, absence of radial symmetry, and lack of discernible pigment network or globular structures. Lesions may display pigmentary variation, irregular surface texture, and lack of telangiectasia. Monitoring these lesions for any changes in size, shape, or color over time is crucial, as their complex and variable nature can make diagnosis challenging even for experienced clinicians. Further diagnostic evaluation and biopsy are necessary to rule out malignancy.
- [Scar Standard]: Scars can exhibit a range of attributes influenced by their underlying causes, encompassing inflammatory and reactive processes to malignancies and dermatosis conditions. Examination of scars should focus on well-defined borders, stable surface quality, and the absence of ulcers, crusts, atrophy, or lichenification. Irregular shapes, varying degrees of redness, pigmentation, vascular changes, and structural patterns may also be observed. Internal architectural patterns like pigment networks, globules, or regression structures are typically absent. When analyzing scars using dermoscopy, it is crucial to consider these core visual features for accurate diagnoses and differentiation among various scar types.
- [Nevus Standard]: Nevi, also known as melanocytic nevi or skin moles, are commonly solitary, well-circumscribed pigmented skin lesions. Their size ranges from approximately 5-6 mm to 1.5 cm in diameter, showcasing key diagnostic features such as a homogeneous, uniform brown to dark brown pigmentation with varying degrees of gradient, from lighter tan to darker brown, accompanied by focal areas of concentrated pigmentation. Irregular borders, scalloped and notched margins, and a gradual transition into surrounding normal skin characterize their shape. The surfaces are smooth and non-scaly, with no evidence of crusting, ulceration, atrophy, lichenification, or telangiectasia. Internally, nevi display a consistently diffused pigment network with evenly dispersed dark brown to black globules and varying degrees of pigment homogeneity. Asymmetric structures and lack of radial symmetry are common. While some nevi may exhibit signs of regression, such as hypopigmentation or whitening, they generally remain stable and do not display active inflammation or malignancy. Monitoring these lesions for any changes in size, shape, or color over time is essential.
- [Melanoma metastasis Standard]: Melanoma metastases can present with a diverse range of morphologic features, including irregular and poorly defined borders, fading transitions, scaling, ulceration, atrophy, and varying degrees of pigmentation. Dermoscopy often reveals heterogeneous pigment distributions, focal pigmented areas, and telangiectasias. Internal structures may be absent, non-uniform, or demonstrate regression or neoplastic components. While some features may resemble benign processes, melanoma metastases consistently display malignant potential. Clinicians should carefully examine and utilize appropriate diagnostic tools for accurate identification and effective management of these life-threatening conditions.
- [Seborrheic keratosis Standard]: Seborrheic keratoses exhibit diverse skin growths with irregular borders, a lack of discernible radial symmetry, and incomplete pigment networks. These benign lesions can resemble more serious conditions, making precise diagnosis crucial. Irregular pigmentation, absence of well-defined borders, and the possibility of regression structures and microhemorrhages necessitate cautious clinical correlation. The variety in color distribution, border contours, and absent pigment networks contribute to an asymmetrical and heterogeneous overall appearance. It is important to note the presence of fine scaling, superficial crusts, and occasional superficial telangiectases, while recognizing the absence of ulceration, atrophy, or lichenification. The lesion's internal architecture lacks discernible radial symmetry or organized patterns like pigment networks, globules, or regression structures, showcasing an asymmetrical, heterogeneous profile that requires careful clinical observation and consideration for potential biopsy. Close examination of the borders, pigmentation, scaling, and internal structure can help differentiate seborrheic keratoses from other similar skin lesions.


